#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084287} :activity/id #uuid "7c5c3245-5a5b-577b-a9ca-fd2370c9f2ee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050211 :topic/id #uuid "36b87c24-96f0-58b5-9017-03b96cf0c512" :topic/math-academy-id 708} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084459 :tutorial/content "When a rational equation contains a quadratic denominator, it's often helpful to factor this denominator first. By doing so, we may spot the least common denominator of the equation, leading to a more straightforward solution. \n\nFor example, consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-1}+\\dfrac{2}{x-2}=\\dfrac{5}{x^2-3x+2}\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side of our equation contains a term with a quadratic denominator. This denominator can be factored, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-1}+\\dfrac{2}{x-2}=\\dfrac{5}{(x-1)(x-2)}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(x-1,\\,x-2,\\,(x-1)(x-2))=(x-1)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The values $x=1$ and $x=2$ *cannot* be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x-2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{2}{x-2}) & =(x-1)(x-2)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{(x-1)(x-2)}) \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)(x-2)}{x-1}+\\frac{2(x-1)(x-2)}{x-2} & =\\frac{5(x-1)(x-2)}{(x-1)(x-2)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}\\cdot (x-2)}{\\cancel{x-1}}+\\frac{2(x-1)\\cdot \\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x-2}} & =\\frac{5\\cancel{(x-1)(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x-2)}} \\\\ (x-2)+2(x-1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)+2(x-1) & =5 \\\\ x-2+2x-2 & =5 \\\\ 3x-4 & =5 \\\\ 3x & =9 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\neq 1$ and $3\\neq 2,$ we conclude that $x=3$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8acc5c11-cb6c-5f97-94fb-077195817e1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3763 :tutorial/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "5840fe27-c9ee-5863-82d0-ba71f16f50ef" :step/math-academy-id 21052 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083241 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083040 :question/id #uuid "ec516fb4-a497-579c-b19d-ebc0a081eb58" :question/math-academy-id "e-5822"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362321}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rationals Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"} :step/id #uuid "1cefe61a-e363-5e65-8b3f-4f1e372a0f50" :step/math-academy-id 21053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083242 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083041 :question/id #uuid "a578b895-ea0e-5d83-aa1d-04a2fe15f332" :question/math-academy-id "e-5824"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049074}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253394} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921